    "image": "assets/library/deck-frame-with-monokote.jpg",
    "imageAlt": "The test frame on a workbench, with purple Monokote covering one wing",
    "photos": [],
    "featured": false,
    "overview": [
      "Before committing to our structural design and final manufacturing, we tested the aircraft to its 3 g limit with a variety of tests using a structural model. We tested the wing assembly, the tail, and the rear landing gear as our main failure points.",
      "## The structural concept",
      "The wing uses thin carbon fiber stringers to carry the wing loading. This is structurally efficient as it maximizes the area moment of inertia of the carbon fiber while using the balsa ribs to keep them from buckling. 3D-printed joints were used to align the rods and as a rule did not carry much load, instead relying on clever carbon fiber member arrangement. I wrote a MATLAB script to find the area moment of inertia for the entire assembly and found that rods thinner than 0.3 mm could handle the bending stress but in reality they were too flexible to assemble. After testing multiple sizes, we decided to use 3 mm circular pultruded carbon rods everywhere. They see about 16 MPa, a safety factor of over 180.",
      "That made the joints, where balsa, printed parts, and epoxy meet, the part to watch. Stress concentrates there and can't be calculated reliably, so we tested them by breaking them. Before building the final aircraft, we made simplified versions of the areas we worried about, and we identified the side-of-body joint, which makes room for the payload, as the most important one. The [fabrication page](project.html?p=sae-micro-fabrication) covers how the joints were built.",
      "![A side-of-body joint loaded to failure, and its CAD section](assets/library/report-side-of-body-joint.png \"A simplified side-of-body joint loaded to failure (left), and a CAD section of the joint (right).\")",
      "## Load tests",
      "The wing was designed for a 3 g limit, a 9 pound load. To check it, we ran a 2 g test: we loaded the frame to 4.5 pounds, which is 1.5 times the aircraft's 3 pound maximum weight, and lifted it by the wingtips. That puts twice the bending moment on the wing compared with flight, and loads the joints to the equivalent of 9 pounds, a 3 g maneuver.",
      "![Two people lifting the loaded wing frame by its wingtips](assets/library/deck-wing-tip-lift-test.jpg \"The wing test: the loaded frame lifted by its wingtips.\")",
      "The tail load required a separate analysis. I created a trim loading method because in a real maneuver most of the force accelerates the aircraft instead of loading the airframe. I worked out the force the tail would need to hold the aircraft trimmed from 1 to 3 g across the expected flight speeds, and the worst case, about 1.1 pounds, became the tail's design load. We tested the tail boom to 2.5 pounds, a safety factor of 2.3, and added a boom support to make it stiffer and avoid buckling.",
      "![A weight hanging from the tail boom during a load test](assets/library/deck-tail-boom-load-test.jpg \"Loading the tail boom. A boom support was added to make it stiffer.\")",
      "## The landing gear failure",
      "The landing gear had two cases: the rear gear had to survive a drop from a 1 foot stall height (3 ft-lb at the wheels), and the front gear had to carry 20% of the weight while taxiing.",
      "The rear landing gear failed after 12 load cycles at the 3 ft-lb test load. The causes were uneven loading, overload from a large stall height, and many load cycles, and the failure was at a single critical point. We strengthened the joints and added redundant structure to eliminate that failure point, and those changes went into the final aircraft.",
      "![The rear landing gear failing at one joint](assets/library/report-landing-gear-failure.jpg \"The rear landing gear failure. It started at one joint, marked as the failure origin.\")"
    ],
