    "overview": [
      "![Diagram of the six mission phases](assets/library/adv-mission-phases.png \"The six phases of the mission: takeoff, transition to vertical flight, delivery, capture, transition to horizontal flight, and landing.\")",
      "The SAE Aero Design Advanced Class is a fully autonomous vertical takeoff and landing (VTOL) challenge. The aircraft must deliver and capture payloads in an 8 ft by 8 ft landing zone, with the aircraft and payload together weighing at most 3.5 lb. {fig:adv-mission-phases} shows the six phases of the mission.",
      "![The aircraft in the air at the airfield, with the T-frame, wing and tail visible](assets/library/adv-mock-run-aircraft-in-air.jpg \"The aircraft in the air during a mock run of the competition on April 8.\")",
      "Note[adv-tilt-mechanism.png \"The tilt mechanism on a front motor, which pivots up for vertical flight and articulates to control yaw.\"]: ## Tricopter configuration\nThe rules allow no more than three motors and propellers, so the aircraft is a tricopter. The two front motors tilt up for the transition to vertical flight and control yaw by articulating, and a third motor at the tail provides lift in vertical flight. The [aircraft trade studies](project.html?p=sae-advanced-frame-trade-study) shows how the team chose this configuration.",
      "The project ran for one full school year, but I spent the fall at my MECOP internship, so I rejoined the team in the spring. From then on I led the mechanical design, project schedule, and design report. {vid:advanced-flight-test} shows a test flight and {vid:advanced-payload-operations} shows the payload operations.",
      "Video: assets/video/advanced-flight-test.mp4 \"A prototype test flight: vertical takeoff, transition to horizontal flight, and vertical landing.\"",
      "Video: assets/video/advanced-payload-operations.mp4 \"Payload operations on a landing mat during testing: the aircraft and two payloads.\" 2x",
      "Stats[top]:\n- 9th | overall, of 22 teams\n- 5th | presentation rank\n- 8th | design report rank",
      "Table[key]: Key results.\n| Metric | Result |\n| Payload capture rate | 95% |\n| Payload standby | 48 hr tested, 6.5% of battery used |\n| Transition distance | 72 to 92 ft (limit: 100 ft) |\n| Hover endurance | 4+ min (30% margin) |\n| Structural design load | 3 g |\n| Flight time logged | 4+ hr |",
      "## Design features",
      "![CAD render of the final Advanced aircraft: a carbon fiber T-frame with a foam wing, ribbed fuselage, two tilting front motors and a tail motor](assets/library/adv-final-cad-render.jpg \"The final aircraft in CAD.\")",
      "- **Structure:** a carbon fiber T-frame, foam aerodynamic shapes (a hot-wire-cut wing and a laser-cut fuselage), and 3D-printed PETG interfaces.\n- **Packs flat:** the aircraft disassembles into the T-frame and the wing, with the landing gear and vertical stabilizer removable, for lay-flat packing and transport.\n- **Autonomy:** ArduPilot's built-in takeoff, landing, and VTOL transition modes with RTK GPS. The payload locates the aircraft with an ultra-wideband module and an OpenMV camera that reads AprilTags on the aircraft for final alignment.",
      "![The final aircraft in CAD, with the payload attached beneath the wing and shown enlarged](assets/library/adv-aircraft-payload-cad.png \"The final aircraft in CAD, with the payload carried beneath the wing and shown enlarged.\")",
      "## The 2026 season",
      "I was part of the original team that pushed to start this project, then left for my internship in the fall. While I was away, the team worked through conceptual design, trade studies, and early flight tests. Early flight tests used a prototype constructed from foam board and wood to validate initial design decisions, hover stability, and autonomous flight, and later prototypes integrated carbon fiber and hot-wire-cut foam. When I rejoined in the spring, I modeled the foam-board prototype in CAD as the starting point for the final aircraft.",
      "I was the lead author of the technical design report. It tracked the design through its life cycle, from setting the design needs and the initial configuration trade studies through testing, and it built on what I learned writing the previous year's report. Once the report was in, I was freed up to finish some of the final testing for the aircraft.",
      "Testing in March showed weak points in the landing gear, the catch mechanism, and the payload lift mechanism. We addressed them in April, along with a redesigned fuselage. Catching problems on the bench, before they could happen in the air, is the part of the project I am proudest of.",
      "Before the competition, on April 8, the team took the aircraft to an airfield for a mock run. The competition ran April 17 to 19, 2026. It was a difficult weekend, and many things went wrong, some of them beyond our control, like the thunderstorm that closed the airfield.",
      "Collage: holding-foam-wing-aircraft.jpg, sae-aero-rain-team.jpg, foam-wing-lab.jpg \"Left to right: a foam-wing aircraft in the shop, carrying the plane through the rain at competition, and the alignment test stand, an early prototype with AprilTags used to test the capture sequence while the other models were built.\"",
      "Table[events]: What went wrong at competition.\n| Event | What happened |\n| Crates | TSA initially refused them, which was an error but still delayed our arrival by 12 hours |\n| Weather | A thunderstorm closed the airfield for most of Saturday |\n| First flight | The aircraft exited the flight boundary by about 6 ft, resulting in a disqualification |\n| Second flight | Rebar in the runway concrete caused a compass calibration error, resulting in a zero-point flight |",
      "The competition did not go the way we wanted. I am so proud of the team and of what we built, and I will always look back on that weekend fondly.",
      "## Takeaways",
      "Callout: ## Optimize before building\nSize and weight are set early and are hard to change later, so optimize the design before building on it.",
      "Callout: ## Design the whole system up front\nTreat the electronics and software as part of the initial design, not as work to finish after the airframe. Gaps left in the initial design become late changes, and each late change requires the aircraft to be retuned and rechecked.",
      "Callout: ## Integrate early\nIntegrating the airframe, electronics, and software takes more time than is often expected, so start integration testing early.",
      "Callout: ## Be ready for anything\nShipping, travel, and site conditions can all derail competition performance. Plan logistics well ahead, and be ready to pivot at a moment's notice.",
      "Callout: ## Share knowledge across the team\nSpread knowledge so that no part of the project depends on one person.",
      "## Next season",
      "The team plans to return. The payload and capture design will be reused, and the aircraft gets a full airframe redesign. See the [2027 season](project.html?p=sae-aero-advanced-2027).",
      "## What I worked on",
      "- [Overview](project.html?p=sae-aero-advanced-vtol): Built the schedule and bill of materials, led weight reduction efforts, and was lead author of the design report.\n- [Aircraft Trade Studies](project.html?p=sae-advanced-frame-trade-study): Built the CAD model, the frame layout, and the part integration.\n- [Wing Sizing & Hot-Wire Cutting](project.html?p=sae-advanced-wings): Chose how much to reduce the wing from flight-test data, and configured and optimized the foam cutter G-code generation.\n- [Fuselage Design](project.html?p=sae-advanced-fuselage): Turned the 3D fuselage shape into flat laser-cut parts.\n- [Integrated Landing Testing](project.html?p=sae-advanced-shock-load-redesign): Conducted mechanical testing, recommended fixes, and retested."
    ],
