    "slug": "sae-advanced-wings",
    "group": "sae-aero",
    "season": 2026,
    "title": "SAE Aero Advanced: Wing Sizing & Hot-Wire Cutting",
    "summary": "Wing sizing from flight transition data, and wing construction.",
    "year": "2025–2026",
    "tags": [
      "Wing sizing",
      "Flight testing",
      "Hot-wire cutting",
      "G-code"
    ],
    "overview": [
      "I led the design, construction, and testing of the aircraft wings. The wing is hot-wire-cut from EPS foam and has carbon fiber spars. It can be removed for travel, as one of the design goals was for the aircraft to pack flat into luggage. That also prompted removable landing gear, which I also worked on. The wing's size comes from flight-test data on how far the aircraft needs to travel to transition from vertical to horizontal flight.",
      "## Sizing the wing around the transition",
      "The transition, not the takeoff, sets the size of the wing. The aircraft must complete its transition from vertical to horizontal flight inside the 200 ft payload operation area. The team set an internal target of 100 ft, half of the area, so that the boundary was never a concern and the aircraft could leave the payload operation area with minimal repositioning.",
      "![Diagram in two parts. Top: one bar per measurement of the transition distance, starting from an aircraft at 0 ft. The payload operation area ends at a hard 200 ft boundary, with the area beyond it marked out of bounds, and a dashed line marks the 100 ft internal target. Before control tuning the transition ran 300 ft, past the boundary. After tuning, the average of three flights was 72 ft. After wing resizing the average was 92 ft. Bottom: the original 1250 mm wing with 100 mm marked at each tip, and the final 1050 mm wing with the same chord](assets/library/adv-wing-resizing.png \"How the wing was resized. After control tuning, three flights averaged 72 ft. The wings were then shortened from 1250 mm to 1050 mm at the same chord, which left 84% of the area and moved the average to 92 ft, against the 100 ft target.\")",
      "A smaller wing weighs less, produces less drag, and is less exposed to wind in vertical flight, but a larger wing lowers the stall speed and shortens the transition. The transition is hard to model, so I measured it: three logged flights averaged 72 ft, found with kinematics as half the end airspeed times the time taken. That was well inside the target, so the wing was larger and heavier than it needed to be.",
      "The relationship between wing area and transition distance called for a wing 28% smaller in area to reach 100 ft. I cut it 16%, shortening the wings from a 49.2 in span to 41.3 in (1250 mm to 1050 mm) at the same chord, which left 84% of the area. That landed at 92 ft, inside the target with margin to spare, and saved 0.05 lb of wing weight.",
      "The handling qualities also improved. The smaller wing was blown around less in the wind, and the aircraft handled better without power (dead stick), especially after the vertical stabilizer was resized.",
      "## Structure and cutting",
      "Later prototypes integrated carbon fiber and hot-wire-cut foam, and the final wing is the result. I used existing hot-wire cutting software and worked out how to take our CAD model through it to generate G-code that cut the wings to the shape we wanted. The software was not written for our custom-built cutter, so I adapted the output, adding start and end G-code to switch the hot wire on and off. I also calibrated the cutter and developed a quick process for making the wings.",
      "The wing has a span of 41.3 in and an aspect ratio of about 4.6, with an Eppler 398 airfoil and a rectangular planform that simplifies manufacturing. Hot-wire-cut EPS foam forms the airfoil around two spars of square carbon fiber tubing, which also mount the wing to the T-frame.",
      "![An airfoil section cut from EPS foam with slots for the spars, next to the uncut foam block, and carbon fiber tubing in square and round sections](assets/library/adv-wing-materials.jpg \"(a) Hot-wire-cut EPS foam: an airfoil section with its spar slots, next to the uncut block. (b) Carbon fiber tubing; the wing's spars are the square tubes.\")",
      "The spars are pultruded carbon, which is less stiff than woven carbon, but the wing can flex, so smaller spars with more deflection were strong enough. Finding the right spar placement and cutouts inside the wing took extensive experimentation."
    ],
