    "slug": "folding-hanger",
    "title": "Folding Hanger",
    "summary": "A folding hanger that goes into a shirt from the top, with a cam channel lock and laser-cut parts, designed as a challenge for myself in high school.",
    "year": "2022",
    "tags": [
      "Product design",
      "Laser cutting",
      "Mechanisms",
      "SolidWorks"
    ],
    "image": "assets/library/folding-hanger-prototype-cad.jpg",
    "imageAlt": "Folding hanger prototype held in front of a laptop showing its CAD model",
    "photos": [],
    "featured": false,
    "overview": [
      "The folding hanger was a design challenge I set for myself in high school. I like hanging up my clothes, but a hanger has to be stretched through a shirt's collar or pushed in from the bottom. I wanted a hanger that folds, so it can go in from the top and then unfold. I could not find anything comparable at the time, so I designed and built one.",
      "## Design",
      "My first design used rubber bands and springs to hold the arms up, and the arms were closed by hand. The weight of a t-shirt was enough to deflect the arms, because the moment is much larger at the ends, so I moved to a mechanical lock.",
      "Collage: folding-hanger-sketch.jpg, 3d-printed-part-black.jpg \"My first concept sketch, and an early 3D-printed prototype that used elastics to hold the arms up.\"",
      "The final mechanism uses a cam channel. The arms and the locking arms rotate about different axes, so each arm tends to rotate into the pins on its locking arm, while the locking arms rotate freely inside the channel. When the hanger closes, the arms fold down. Designing it took a large linkage sketch in CAD, and it taught me about cam joints. The motion study ({vid:hanger-motion-study-1}) shows the mechanism folding, and the t-shirt video ({vid:hanger-t-shirt}) shows it working on a real shirt.",
      "I modeled every part in SolidWorks and assembled them with motion studies, so I could check that the arms, the locking arms, and the cam channel move through their full range without interfering before anything was cut.",
      "Video: assets/video/hanger-t-shirt.mp4 \"The folding hanger on a t-shirt.\"",
      "Video: assets/video/hanger-motion-study-1.mp4 \"A SolidWorks motion study of the hanger folding.\" 2x",
      "Collage: folding-hanger-on-white-shirt.jpg, folding-hanger-on-black-shirt.jpg \"The final hanger in use on a white shirt and a black shirt.\"",
      "Callout: ## Stress relief\nA slit in the bottom of each locking arm holds a rubber band that helps the arm lock into place. The slit started a crack at its tip, so the final design ends the slit with a round hole, a stress-relief hole. This is the same principle as stop drilling a fatigue crack in an aircraft structure.",
      "## Manufacturing",
      "The mechanism needs tight tolerances. I laser cut calibration sheets with slightly different hole diameters to find which diameter gave a clearance fit and which gave an interference fit, and I used those diameters in the final design.",
      "Collage: laser-cut-parts-table.jpg, folding-hanger-parts-box.jpg, folding-hanger-calibration-sheets.jpg \"Laser-cut hanger parts laid out on a table, a box of cut parts, and the wood and acrylic calibration sheets with holes and slots of graduated sizes, used to choose the final hole diameters.\"",
      "- The arms are 1/8 in pine, and the locking arms are 3 mm acrylic, which is slightly thinner, so they slide freely in the channel.\n- The parts are nested to use less material.\n- The joints are interference-fit pins made of the same wood as the arms, with no glue. The small amount of give is what makes the mechanism work, and glue would make it fail.",
      "The project ended when the school shop closed for the summer."
    ],
